1. Java Persistence API (JPA)

- JPA (Java Persistence API) is the standard Java specification used for mapping Java objects to relational databases.
- It provides a common set of interfaces and annotations for performing database operations.
- JPA itself is only a specification; it does not contain any implementation.
- Different ORM frameworks such as Hibernate, EclipseLink, and OpenJPA implement the JPA specification.

Common JPA Imports

import javax.persistence.Entity;
import javax.persistence.Table;
import javax.persistence.Id;
import javax.persistence.EntityManager;
import javax.persistence.EntityTransaction;
Example Entity
@Entity
@Table(name = "employee")
public class Employee {

    @Id
    private int id;

    private String name;
}
2. Hibernate
Hibernate is a widely used ORM framework that implements the JPA specification.
It automatically converts Java objects into database records and vice versa.
Hibernate can be used either through JPA or by using its own native API.
It manages SQL generation, database connections, caching, lazy loading, and transaction handling.
Hibernate Native API Example
Session session = sessionFactory.openSession();
Transaction transaction = session.beginTransaction();

session.save(employee);

transaction.commit();
session.close();
Core Hibernate Components
Component	Description
SessionFactory	Creates Session objects and is shared throughout the application.
Session	Used to perform CRUD operations with the database.
Transaction	Ensures database operations are executed safely as a single unit.
ConnectionProvider	Supplies JDBC connections to Hibernate.
Spring Data JPA Repository
@Repository
public interface EmployeeRepository extends JpaRepository<Employee, Integer> {

}
Extending JpaRepository automatically provides CRUD methods.
No need to manually write SQL or Hibernate session code for common operations.
Hibernate Approach (Traditional)
public Integer saveEmployee(Employee employee) {

    Session session = sessionFactory.openSession();
    Transaction transaction = null;
    Integer id = null;

    try {
        transaction = session.beginTransaction();

        id = (Integer) session.save(employee);

        transaction.commit();
    }
    catch (HibernateException e) {

        if (transaction != null) {
            transaction.rollback();
        }

        e.printStackTrace();
    }
    finally {
        session.close();
    }

    return id;
}
Spring Data JPA Approach
Repository
public interface EmployeeRepository extends JpaRepository<Employee, Integer> {

}
Service Layer
@Service
public class EmployeeService {

    @Autowired
    private EmployeeRepository repository;

    @Transactional
    public void saveEmployee(Employee employee) {

        repository.save(employee);

    }
}

Advantages:

Minimal coding effort
Automatic transaction handling
Built-in CRUD methods
Cleaner and easier-to-maintain code
